<svelte:head>
	<title>Codex — Pontia</title>
	<meta name="description" content="Set up the Codex daemon for Pontia on Linux and macOS." />
</svelte:head>

# Codex

Pontia connects to Codex's app-server daemon. Install and authenticate Codex as the same user who runs Pontia, then check daemon support:

```sh
codex app-server daemon start --help
codex app-server daemon version --help
```

Codex uses its own model credentials, sandbox, and approval policy. Choose a policy appropriate for the work you will run from the Dashboard.

## Interactive setup

Run `pontia init` and select **Codex**. Pontia configures automatic startup, verifies the connection, and saves the Codex home for both services.

<h2 id="manual-setup">Manual setup</h2>

For non-interactive initialization, create or update the service definition for your platform below. Use absolute paths for the executable and Codex home. Find your executable with `command -v codex`; if it needs Node, include Node's directory in the service's `PATH`.

### Linux

Create `~/.config/systemd/user/` if needed, then save this as `pontia-codex.service` inside it:

```ini
[Unit]
Description=Codex App Server Daemon Startup
After=network.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart="/absolute/path/to/codex" app-server daemon start
Environment="CODEX_HOME=/home/you/.codex"

[Install]
WantedBy=default.target
```

Enable and check automatic startup:

```sh
systemctl --user daemon-reload
loginctl enable-linger "$(id -un)"
systemctl --user enable --now pontia-codex.service
systemctl --user is-enabled pontia-codex.service
```

Lingering keeps user services available after logout and may require administrator authorization. For logs, use `journalctl --user -u pontia-codex.service`.

### macOS

Create `~/Library/LaunchAgents/` if needed, then save this as `dev.pontia.codex-daemon.plist` inside it:

```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>dev.pontia.codex-daemon</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/absolute/path/to/codex</string>
    <string>app-server</string><string>daemon</string><string>start</string>
  </array>
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>CODEX_HOME</key><string>/Users/you/.codex</string>
    <key>PATH</key><string>/absolute/path/to/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin</string>
  </dict>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key><true/>
</dict>
</plist>
```

Validate and load it in your logged-in user's GUI domain:

```sh
plutil -lint "$HOME/Library/LaunchAgents/dev.pontia.codex-daemon.plist"
launchctl enable "gui/$(id -u)/dev.pontia.codex-daemon"
launchctl bootstrap "gui/$(id -u)" "$HOME/Library/LaunchAgents/dev.pontia.codex-daemon.plist"
launchctl print "gui/$(id -u)/dev.pontia.codex-daemon"
```

To replace a loaded definition, run `launchctl bootout "gui/$(id -u)/dev.pontia.codex-daemon"` before `bootstrap`.

## Verify the daemon

Use the same Codex home as your service definition:

```sh
CODEX_HOME="${CODEX_HOME:-$HOME/.codex}" codex app-server daemon version
```

A running daemon returns JSON with `"status": "running"`. Continue with [initialization](/docs/initialization#start-the-local-service) to start Pontia, match its Codex home as described below, enable Codex, and connect to the Remote Dashboard.

<h2 id="pontia-codex-home">Match Pontia's Codex home</h2>

After the first `pontia up`, check that Pontia's service definition saves the same absolute `CODEX_HOME` as the Codex daemon. This applies to both the default `~/.codex` directory and custom homes. Add or update the value as needed, retaining the other service settings:

| Platform | Pontia definition | Change | Apply |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Linux | `~/.config/systemd/user/pontia.service` | Add `Environment="CODEX_HOME=/your/path"` under `[Service]` | `pontia up` |
| macOS | `~/Library/LaunchAgents/dev.pontia.pontiad.plist` | Add a `CODEX_HOME` string to `EnvironmentVariables` | `pontia down`, then `pontia up` |

On macOS, keep the Pontia definition as XML and insert or update this entry inside `EnvironmentVariables`, preserving the line break and four-space indentation shown:

```xml
    <key>CODEX_HOME</key>
    <string>/your/path</string>
```

Substitute your selected path for `/your/path`, escape XML characters such as `&`, and retain the layout of the surrounding entries. An existing matching configuration is ready to use.

After a change, reload the service using the command in the table. Check the saved `CODEX_HOME` and confirm that `pontia status` reports a healthy service, then enable Codex. Saving the path in the service definition makes it available to Pontia's background process.
